  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Visual Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now diving into the user\'s feedback concerning the visual appeal of the hero section. Specifically, the request focuses on incorporating a \"fillete\" (likely a fillet or similar element) and dessert, while keeping the original plate and tray concept. My focus is on understanding the desired aesthetic changes to make it visually attractive. I\'m exploring how to translate this feedback into specific design elements.\n\n\n**Refining Aesthetic Details**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the details necessary to make the visual elements look far more professional. The goal is to elevate the \"hero\" section\'s 3D assets to a high-end standard. My current plan involves detailed, Three.js-based procedural modeling of the fillet and dessert, with an emphasis on realistic textures, lighting, and materials, like bump and roughness maps. The plate, tray, or cloche will also require very detailed renderings, to be arranged harmoniously and elegantly.\n\n\n**Deconstructing 3D Composition**\n\nNow, I\'m focusing on the `Hero3D.tsx` component, dissecting the 3D composition into individual objects. Dish 1, the centerpiece, will showcase the Filete Wellington on risotto. This will use ceramic and metal materials. Dish 2, positioned top right, will feature a silver tray under a glass cloche.\n\n\n**Visualizing Refinements**\n\nI\'m now outlining Dish 2\'s components. I envision a glass cloche with a gold knob, refracting light beautifully. Inside, I\'m considering a warm appetizer or a delicate mist effect. Dish 3 will feature a Cr\303\250me Br\303\273l\303\251e in a fluted ramekin, topped with berries and mint. I\'m focusing on procedural textures for the meat, caramel, and risotto to achieve maximum realism directly in Three.js, without any external image downloads.\n\n\n**Implementing Textures & Lighting**\n\nI\'m now implementing the lighting setup within the `Hero3D.tsx` component. I\'m focusing on a warm studio environment with specific light colors: a soft ambient light and a warm key light for soft PCF shadows. I\'m also adding a warm rim light and a cool fill light to balance the scene. I am creating procedural canvas textures for the pastry, caramelized sugar, and marble. I will start by implementing a base function for creating procedural canvas textures.\n\n\n**Generating Procedural Textures**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `createPastryTexture` function. This involves generating a golden-brown base, then superimposing a cross-hatch pattern with a darker brown for the pastry. Finally, I\'m adding subtle golden highlights to create a baked effect. This procedural method is much cleaner than using downloaded images. I am then creating the `createCaramelTexture` function. I will use this as a base for other procedural textures.\n\n\n**Crafting Caramel & Pastry**\n\nI\'m currently working on generating procedural caramel textures using a radial gradient with dark veins. For the pastry, I\'m employing a golden-brown base and a cross-hatch pattern with subtle highlights to simulate a baked effect. I\'m focusing on the meat textures and overall visual quality.\n\n\n**Detailing Wellington Components**\n\nI\'m now detailing the Wellington components. I will create a dark rim layer using mushroom duxelles between the pastry and beef. Underneath, I\'ll place a white/cream-textured cylinder for the risotto bed, and a dark, glossy red-wine reduction puddle for the sauce. Finally, a rosemary sprig will be added, a thin brown stem with tiny green leaves attached along it. The dish should feel very authentic.\n\n\n\n"
